<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-4-4-data-analysis">
  <time minutes="90" />
  <title>Data Analysis</title>

  <idx>data-analysis</idx>
  <introduction>
    <p>Quick Links:
      <xref text="global" ref="learning-from-data, learning-from-metadata,
      algorithms-for-data-analysis, LOs-4-4, vocab-4-4"/>
    </p>
    <p>
      In this topic, we analyze how people learn from data and metadata,
      and how programmers design algorithms and code for data analysis.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="learning-from-data">
    <title>What People Can Learn from Data</title>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.A.1</title>
      <p>
        People use data-driven features every day, such as feeds, dashboards, recommendations,
        and filters. Many apps generate annual profiles by organizing a user's activity into
        categories such as top items, totals, and trends over time, revealing patterns in how
        the user engaged with the app.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.A.2</title>
      <p>
        Data are raw values or observations. Information is what people learn from data after
        analyzing it to identify patterns or meaning. One common process is to collect or select
        data, clean it if needed, then transform, filter, aggregate, and visualize it to answer
        questions and reveal patterns.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.A.3</title>
      <p>
        Depending on how data is collected, it may not be uniform. When people enter data into an
        open text field, they may represent the same idea in different ways (using abbreviations,
        different spellings, or different capitalization). Real-world data is often messy because
        people use different terms, type differently, or make mistakes.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.A.4</title>
      <p>
        Cleaning data is the process of making data more consistent and usable without changing its
        meaning, such as standardizing equivalent entries like "1/5," "01-05," and "Jan 5" to a
        single agreed-upon date format.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.A.5</title>
      <p>
        Filtering data keeps only the items that meet a condition so patterns are easier to see,
        such as filtering for trips under ten miles or survey responses that selected "strongly agree".
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.A.6</title>
      <p>
        Aggregating data combines many values into a summary, such as counting how many items meet
        a condition or computing a total or average. Comparing data values can identify key results,
        such as finding the maximum or minimum value or determining which category occurs most often.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.A.7</title>
      <p>
        Visualizations, such as tables, charts, and graphs, communicate insights by making trends
        and comparisons easier to notice than raw data alone. Different visual formats highlight
        different patterns.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.A.8</title>
      <p>
        Tools like spreadsheets can efficiently organize data, apply filters, sort records, and
        compute summaries, making it easier to explore patterns quickly.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.A.9</title>
      <p>
        Programmers should choose data processes and visualizations that match user goals and avoid
        misleading conclusions. This includes using clear labels, selecting a visualization that fits
        the type of data and the question being asked, and ensuring filters match what users want to compare.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="learning-from-metadata">
    <title>What People Can Learn from Metadata</title>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.B.1</title>
      <p>
        Metadata are data about data. They describe properties of a digital item, such as when it
        was created, who created it, where it came from, its type or format, its size, or what
        device or app produced it.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.B.2</title>
      <p>
        Changing metadata does not change the primary data. Editing a photo's date taken, for
        instance, does not alter the image itself. However, changing metadata can affect how the
        data is searched, sorted, grouped, or interpreted.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.B.3</title>
      <p>
        Metadata helps systems find, organize, and manage information by providing labels and
        structure, such as titles, tags, categories, timestamps, or file types.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.B.4</title>
      <p>
        Metadata adds context that makes data more useful for analysis, such as identifying the
        source of information, when it was recorded, or settings used to collect it, which helps
        people and programs compare data and interpret results more accurately.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.B.5</title>
      <p>
        People can use metadata to detect patterns or support conclusions without examining the full
        content, such as how often events occur or how items can be grouped or filtered.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.B.6</title>
      <p>
        Programmers use metadata to support user tasks such as sorting by most recent, filtering by
        location, and organizing items into groups like favorites. Clear metadata and labels help
        users find what they need quickly and reduce confusion or mistakes.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="algorithms-for-data-analysis">
    <title>Algorithms and Code for Data Analysis</title>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.C.1</title>
      <p>
        Programs can analyze data to reveal patterns, such as counts, totals, averages, trends,
        comparisons, and unusual values. This helps people answer questions and make decisions
        based on evidence.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.C.2</title>
      <p>
        To analyze data with a program, programmers often begin by stating a question or goal and
        defining what counts as relevant data and correct output. They then choose processes that
        match the goal, such as transforming values, filtering records, combining values to compute
        summaries, or comparing items to find a best match or extreme.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.C.3</title>
      <p>
        Programmers verify extracted information by checking sample rows, comparing against a small
        hand-checked subset, and confirming that filters and transformations match intended results
        and the conclusions match the data.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.4.C.4</title>
      <p>
        Many data analysis programs follow a common pattern: traverse a data set, apply a condition
        to keep or skip items, update an accumulator (such as a count, total, or maximum), and then
        produce output (a summary value, a new filtered collection, or a visualization-ready result).
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="LOs-4-4">
    <title>Learning Objectives Review</title>

    <remark>
      <title>4.4.A</title>
      <p>
        Determine what people can learn from analyzing data.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>4.4.B</title>
      <p>
        Determine what people can learn from metadata.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>4.4.C</title>
      <p>
        Develop algorithms and code to analyze data.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="vocab-4-4">
   <title>Vocabulary Review</title>
  </subsection>
</section>
